1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,110
Claude Fable 5.2:

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:04,360
you think safety must cost you a weaker model,

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:05,320
but Anthropic says

4
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:08,332
it's actually the same underlying model as Mythos 5.2.

5
00:00:08,582 --> 00:00:11,379
Here's how: our example, 10 requests.

6
00:00:11,629 --> 00:00:14,260
Before, with no checker strong enough,

7
00:00:14,370 --> 00:00:16,860
Mythos Preview went to a small group of cyber defenders

8
00:00:16,970 --> 00:00:18,220
and infrastructure providers.

9
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:19,626
You got a weaker one.

10
00:00:19,876 --> 00:00:22,810
Now, a separate AI checker reads our requests first.

11
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:24,809
Nine pass to the full model.

12
00:00:25,059 --> 00:00:26,725
One asks for attack code... blocked!

13
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:28,320
In Claude apps,

14
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,550
an older Opus model usually answers,

15
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:32,797
and you're told.

16
00:00:33,047 --> 00:00:34,400
In Anthropic's early tests,

17
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,000
5.2's checker blocked about 55% fewer

18
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,805
requests than 5.1's.

19
00:00:39,055 --> 00:00:41,146
So fewer requests leave the full model.

20
00:00:41,396 --> 00:00:41,650
So, what we learned:

21
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:45,439
in our example, all 10 once got a weaker model.

22
00:00:45,689 --> 00:00:46,300
Now 9 reach the full model;

23
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:48,860
in Claude apps, the checker usually

24
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:51,750
sends the blocked one to older Opus.

25
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:52,850
Want more?

26
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:55,503
Subscribe, and comment what we should explain next.
